1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,699
This is MATH 239, video 3. The topic is bijections and bijective proofs.

2
00:00:07,149 --> 00:00:13,165
After this video, you can say when a function is injective, surjective or bijective.

3
00:00:13,615 --> 00:00:18,585
You can use two inverse functions to prove that two sets have the same size.

4
00:00:19,035 --> 00:00:23,089
You can write a bijective proof in the form the course expects.

5
00:00:23,539 --> 00:00:34,312
And you can solve practice problem P1-6, assignment questions A1-1 and A1-3, and Practice Midterm question 2.

6
00:00:34,762 --> 00:00:47,391
In video 1, we turned one kind of object into another kind to count it. Now we make this idea exact. The problem: show that two sets have the same size, without counting them.

7
00:00:47,841 --> 00:00:50,657
Here are the 8 subsets of 1, 2, 3.

8
00:00:51,107 --> 00:00:55,353
Here are the 8 vectors of length 3 with entries 0 and 1.

9
00:00:55,803 --> 00:00:59,408
The empty set goes to the vector 0, 0, 0.

10
00:00:59,858 --> 00:01:07,879
The subset with only 1 goes to 1, 0, 0. Each element i of the subset puts a 1 in position i.

11
00:01:08,329 --> 00:01:15,433
For example, the subset 1, 3 puts 1s in positions 1 and 3. So it goes to 1, 0, 1.

12
00:01:15,883 --> 00:01:21,366
The other subsets work in the same way. Each subset gets exactly one arrow out.

13
00:01:21,816 --> 00:01:28,493
Every vector gets exactly one arrow in. No vector gets two arrows, and no vector is missed.

14
00:01:28,943 --> 00:01:34,319
So we paired each element of the left set with exactly one element of the right set.

15
00:01:34,769 --> 00:01:43,644
Such a perfect pairing is called a bijection. If it exists, the two sets have the same size. We do not have to count either set.

16
00:01:44,094 --> 00:01:54,121
A function f from A to B sends each element a of A to one element f of a in B. So in each picture, each a has exactly one arrow out.

17
00:01:54,571 --> 00:02:04,960
f is surjective if every b in B is the image of at least one a. So every b has at least one arrow in. The middle picture is surjective.

18
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:16,226
f is injective if f of a equals f of a prime only when a equals a prime. So no b has two arrows in. The left picture is injective.

19
00:02:16,676 --> 00:02:22,052
The left picture is not surjective. The circled element of B has no arrow in.

20
00:02:22,502 --> 00:02:28,113
The middle picture is not injective. The circled element of B has two arrows in.

21
00:02:28,563 --> 00:02:38,141
f is bijective if it is injective and surjective. Then every b has exactly one arrow in. The right picture is a bijection.

22
00:02:38,591 --> 00:02:43,157
The old names are one-to-one for injective, and onto for surjective.

23
00:02:43,607 --> 00:02:51,031
Here is a bijection f from A to B. Because f is surjective, every b is the image of at least one a.

24
00:02:51,481 --> 00:02:56,259
Because f is injective, every b is the image of at most one a.

25
00:02:56,709 --> 00:03:03,387
So every b is the image of exactly one a. The green numbers count the arrows into each b.

26
00:03:03,837 --> 00:03:08,231
So the arrows pair off all elements of A with all elements of B.

27
00:03:08,681 --> 00:03:12,969
So A and B have the same number of elements. Here both have 5.

28
00:03:13,419 --> 00:03:17,579
The notes write A, double arrow, B, when a bijection exists.

29
00:03:18,029 --> 00:03:24,856
The converse is also true: two finite sets of the same size always have a bijection between them.

30
00:03:25,306 --> 00:03:36,378
Proposition 1.11 gives the tool we use in every bijective proof. Take a function f from A to B, and a function g from B to A.

31
00:03:36,828 --> 00:03:43,036
Assume that g undoes f. That is, g of f of a equals a, for every a in A.

32
00:03:43,486 --> 00:03:50,953
Also assume that f undoes g. That is, f of g of b equals b, for every b in B.

33
00:03:51,403 --> 00:03:56,288
Then f is injective. Suppose that f of a equals f of a prime.

34
00:03:56,738 --> 00:04:02,583
Apply g to both sides. This gives g of f of a equals g of f of a prime.

35
00:04:03,033 --> 00:04:09,753
By the first assumption, the left side is a, and the right side is a prime. So a equals a prime.

36
00:04:10,203 --> 00:04:14,833
And f is surjective. Take any b in B. Let a be g of b.

37
00:04:15,283 --> 00:04:22,515
Then f of a is f of g of b. By the second assumption, this is b. So b has an arrow in.

38
00:04:22,965 --> 00:04:29,983
So f is a bijection. The same two lines, with f and g swapped, show that g is a bijection.

39
00:04:30,433 --> 00:04:40,993
We call f and g mutually inverse bijections, and write g equals f inverse. The notes leave this proof as Exercise 1.4 b.

40
00:04:41,443 --> 00:04:50,446
A bijective proof that A and B have the same size has four parts. Part 1: define a function f from A to B by a clear rule.

41
00:04:50,896 --> 00:05:04,528
Part 2: show that f of a is in B, for every a in A. A rule can give an object that is not in B. So check every condition in the definition of B. Students often forget this part.

42
00:05:04,978 --> 00:05:11,506
Part 3: define the inverse function g from B to A, and check that g of b is in A.

43
00:05:11,956 --> 00:05:28,681
Part 4: show both round trips. g of f of a equals a, and f of g of b equals b. Then Proposition 1.11 says that f is a bijection. You do not need separate proofs of injective and surjective.

44
00:05:29,131 --> 00:05:39,777
Conclude that A and B have the same size. Assignment question A1-3 part c, and Practice Midterm question 2, ask for these parts.

45
00:05:40,227 --> 00:05:48,141
Example 1.12 makes the first slide exact. P of n is the set of all subsets of 1 to n.

46
00:05:48,591 --> 00:05:54,885
0 1 to the n is the set of all vectors of length n with entries 0 or 1.

47
00:05:55,335 --> 00:05:58,876
Here n is 8, and S is 2, 3, 5, 7.

48
00:05:59,326 --> 00:06:07,390
The map alpha sends a subset S to a vector. Entry i is 1 if i is in S, and 0 if i is not in S.

49
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,955
Positions 1, 2 and 3 give 0, 1, 1.

50
00:06:11,405 --> 00:06:15,479
Positions 4 to 8 give 0, 1, 0, 1, 0.

51
00:06:15,929 --> 00:06:22,009
So alpha of S is the vector 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0.

52
00:06:22,459 --> 00:06:29,371
The inverse map reads the vector back. S of alpha is the set of positions i where the entry is 1.

53
00:06:29,821 --> 00:06:37,053
The 1s are in positions 2, 3, 5 and 7. This gives S again. Each map undoes the other.

54
00:06:37,503 --> 00:06:51,647
So by Proposition 1.11, the two sets have the same size. A vector has 2 choices in each of the 8 positions. So there are 2 to the 8, which is 256, vectors.

55
00:06:52,097 --> 00:07:01,868
In general, there are 2 to the n vectors, so there are 2 to the n subsets. This is Theorem 1.3, now proved with a bijection.

56
00:07:02,318 --> 00:07:14,222
Example 1.13 writes the proof of Theorem 1.9 as a bijection. M of n and t is the set of multisets of size n with t types.

57
00:07:14,672 --> 00:07:24,400
B of n plus t minus 1 and t minus 1 is the set of t minus 1 element subsets of 1 to n plus t minus 1.

58
00:07:24,850 --> 00:07:31,485
Here n is 10 and t is 4. So there are 13 positions, and the subsets have 3 elements.

59
00:07:31,935 --> 00:07:38,505
Take the 3 element subset S equals 2, 7, 11. Mark these positions with crosses.

60
00:07:38,955 --> 00:07:43,094
Sort them: s 1 is 2, s 2 is 7, s 3 is 11.

61
00:07:43,544 --> 00:07:51,523
Add two posts: s 0 equals 0 on the left, and s 4 equals n plus t, which is 14, on the right.

62
00:07:51,973 --> 00:07:57,733
The other positions get circles. There are 13 minus 3, which is 10, circles.

63
00:07:58,183 --> 00:08:05,692
The crosses cut the circles into 4 runs. The differences of neighbouring posts are 2, 5, 4 and 3.

64
00:08:06,142 --> 00:08:20,115
Subtract 1 from each difference, because the run does not include the cross at its right end. So m i equals s i minus s i minus 1, minus 1. This gives 1, 4, 3, 2.

65
00:08:20,565 --> 00:08:25,920
Each m i is at least 0, because s i minus 1 is less than s i.

66
00:08:26,370 --> 00:08:38,402
The sum of the m i is s 4 minus 4, which is 14 minus 4, which is 10. In the picture, 1 plus 4 plus 3 plus 2 equals 10, which is n.

67
00:08:38,852 --> 00:08:49,561
So mu equals 1, 4, 3, 2 is a multiset of size 10 with 4 types. This is part 2 of the proof: the image is in M of n and t.

68
00:08:50,011 --> 00:08:55,174
For the inverse, start with a multiset mu. Here mu is 1, 4, 3, 2.

69
00:08:55,624 --> 00:09:07,400
Write m 1 circles, then a cross, then m 2 circles, and so on. This gives 1 circle, a cross, 4 circles, a cross, 3 circles, a cross, and 2 circles.

70
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:21,247
Cross number i sits at position s i. Up to cross i, there are m 1 plus up to m i circles, and i crosses. So s i equals m 1 plus up to m i, plus i.

71
00:09:21,697 --> 00:09:26,689
s 1 equals m 1 plus 1, which is 1 plus 1, which is 2.

72
00:09:27,139 --> 00:09:33,774
s 2 equals m 1 plus m 2, plus 2. That is 1 plus 4 plus 2, which is 7.

73
00:09:34,224 --> 00:09:43,269
s 3 equals m 1 plus m 2 plus m 3, plus 3. That is 1 plus 4 plus 3 plus 3, which is 11.

74
00:09:43,719 --> 00:09:46,770
So we get back S equals 2, 7, 11.

75
00:09:47,220 --> 00:10:03,476
In general, each s i is larger than the one before, because we add m i plus 1. The last cross is at most n plus t minus 1. So S is a t minus 1 element subset of 1 to n plus t minus 1.

76
00:10:03,926 --> 00:10:18,667
The two maps undo each other. The notes leave this check as Exercise 1.6. So the two sets have the same size, n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1. This proves Theorem 1.9.

77
00:10:19,117 --> 00:10:36,269
For n equals 10 and t equals 4, this is 13 choose 3. That is 13 times 12 times 11, over 3 times 2 times 1, which is 286. A computer check lists all 286 multisets.

78
00:10:36,719 --> 00:10:47,962
Here is practice problem P1-6 from Assignment 1. The picture shows a function f from A to B. Is it injective, surjective, or bijective?

79
00:10:48,412 --> 00:10:54,193
A has 7 elements, a 1 to a 7. B has 7 elements, b 1 to b 7.

80
00:10:54,643 --> 00:10:56,286
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:10:56,736 --> 00:11:08,981
b 7 has two arrows in: one from a 5 and one from a 7. So f of a 5 equals f of a 7, but a 5 is not a 7. So f is not injective.

82
00:11:09,431 --> 00:11:16,493
b 3 has no arrow in. No a has f of a equal to b 3. So f is not surjective.

83
00:11:16,943 --> 00:11:21,295
So f is not bijective. This agrees with the posted answer.

84
00:11:21,745 --> 00:11:31,089
A and B have the same size, 7. Equal size says that some bijection exists. It does not say that a given function is one.

85
00:11:31,539 --> 00:11:44,317
Assignment question A1-1 asks for a bijective proof. For n at least 1, the sum of n choose k over even k equals the sum of n choose k over odd k.

86
00:11:44,767 --> 00:11:46,410
Pause the video and try it.

87
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:59,831
First, find two sets that the two sides count. n choose k counts the k element subsets of 1 to n. So let A be the subsets of even size, and B the subsets of odd size.

88
00:12:00,281 --> 00:12:09,433
Take n equals 3. Column k holds the subsets of size k. There are 3 choose k of them: 1, 3, 3 and 1.

89
00:12:09,883 --> 00:12:15,643
The even sizes are 0 and 2. They give 1 plus 3, which is 4 subsets.

90
00:12:16,093 --> 00:12:21,426
The odd sizes are 1 and 3. They give 3 plus 1, which is also 4.

91
00:12:21,876 --> 00:12:39,519
In general, a subset has exactly one size. So A is a disjoint union of the sets of k element subsets, for even k. Its size is the left side. In the same way, the size of B is the right side. Now we need a bijection from A to B.

92
00:12:39,969 --> 00:12:44,641
The map toggles the element 1. If 1 is not in S, add 1.

93
00:12:45,091 --> 00:12:47,395
If 1 is in S, remove 1.

94
00:12:47,845 --> 00:12:56,250
Take n equals 3. The empty set does not contain 1. So f adds 1, and f of the empty set is the set 1.

95
00:12:56,700 --> 00:13:00,668
1, 2 contains 1. So f removes 1, and gives 2.

96
00:13:01,118 --> 00:13:05,235
1, 3 contains 1. So f removes 1, and gives 3.

97
00:13:05,685 --> 00:13:10,656
2, 3 does not contain 1. So f adds 1, and gives 1, 2, 3.

98
00:13:11,106 --> 00:13:22,519
Part 2: f of S is in B. The map adds or removes exactly one element. So the size changes by 1, and an even size becomes an odd size.

99
00:13:22,969 --> 00:13:39,460
Part 3 and 4: apply the toggle twice. The first toggle adds 1 and the second removes it, or the other way. So f of f of S equals S. For example, f of f of the empty set is f of 1, which is the empty set.

100
00:13:39,910 --> 00:13:47,675
So the same rule, from B to A, is the inverse. By Proposition 1.11, f is a bijection.

101
00:13:48,125 --> 00:13:52,243
Why do we need n at least 1? The map uses the element 1.

102
00:13:52,693 --> 00:14:04,767
For n equals 0, the only subset is the empty set. Then the even sum is 0 choose 0, which is 1, and the odd sum is 0. So the identity is false.

103
00:14:05,217 --> 00:14:13,815
Here is the solution in the form a marker expects. First, define the two sets, and say why their sizes are the two sides.

104
00:14:14,265 --> 00:14:20,963
Second, define the map. Say that it uses the element 1, which exists because n is at least 1.

105
00:14:21,413 --> 00:14:29,413
Third, show that f sends A into B. The size changes by exactly 1. The same rule sends B into A.

106
00:14:29,863 --> 00:14:35,325
Fourth, show that the maps are mutually inverse: f of f of S equals S.

107
00:14:35,775 --> 00:14:43,156
Cite Proposition 1.11, and conclude that A and B have the same size. That is the identity.

108
00:14:43,606 --> 00:14:52,097
Assignment question A1-3. A n is the set of 0 1 vectors of length n with exactly two 1s.

109
00:14:52,547 --> 00:15:01,720
B n is the set of integer pairs b 1, b 2, with 0 at most b 1, at most b 2, at most n minus 2.

110
00:15:02,170 --> 00:15:05,989
Find a bijection and prove it. Pause the video and try it.

111
00:15:06,439 --> 00:15:15,207
Let the two 1s be in positions i and j, with i less than j. Define f of a as i minus 1, j minus 2.

112
00:15:15,657 --> 00:15:26,259
Take the second vector, for n equals 5. Its 1s are in positions 1 and 3. So f gives 1 minus 1, 3 minus 2, which is 0, 1.

113
00:15:26,709 --> 00:15:32,832
The other vectors with i equal to 1 give 0 0, 0 2 and 0 3.

114
00:15:33,282 --> 00:15:39,234
The other six vectors give 1 1, 1 2, 1 3, 2 2, 2 3 and 3 3.

115
00:15:39,684 --> 00:15:45,551
A 5 has 5 choose 2 vectors. That is 5 times 4 over 2, which is 10.

116
00:15:46,001 --> 00:15:54,747
The 10 images are exactly the 10 pairs of B 5, each one time. A computer check confirms this for n up to 9.

117
00:15:55,197 --> 00:16:03,795
Now part c, the proof. Here is a vector of length 7. Its 1s are in positions i equals 2 and j equals 5.

118
00:16:04,245 --> 00:16:08,703
f of a is 2 minus 1, 5 minus 2, which is 1, 3.

119
00:16:09,153 --> 00:16:17,601
Part 2: show that f of a is in B n, for every a. The 1s are in positions i less than j, between 1 and n.

120
00:16:18,051 --> 00:16:32,985
Because i and j are integers, i less than j means i is at most j minus 1. So 1 is at most i, i is at most j minus 1, and j minus 1 is at most n minus 1.

121
00:16:33,435 --> 00:16:46,533
Subtract 1 from each part. So 0 is at most i minus 1, which is at most j minus 2, which is at most n minus 2. These are exactly the conditions of B n.

122
00:16:46,983 --> 00:16:59,143
Part 3: the inverse g puts 1s in positions b 1 plus 1 and b 2 plus 2. For 1, 3, that is 1 plus 1, which is 2, and 3 plus 2, which is 5.

123
00:16:59,593 --> 00:17:10,516
Because b 1 is at most b 2, b 1 plus 1 is less than b 2 plus 2. Both positions are between 1 and n. So g of b is in A n.

124
00:17:10,966 --> 00:17:21,931
Part 4: g of f of a has 1s in positions i minus 1 plus 1, which is i, and j minus 2 plus 2, which is j. So it is a.

125
00:17:22,381 --> 00:17:29,635
f of g of b is b 1 plus 1 minus 1, and b 2 plus 2 minus 2. So it is b.

126
00:17:30,085 --> 00:17:33,989
By Proposition 1.11, f is a bijection.

127
00:17:34,439 --> 00:17:40,796
Practice Midterm question 2. A n is the set of 3 element subsets of 1 to n.

128
00:17:41,246 --> 00:17:48,606
B n is the set of triples of positive integers b 1, b 2, b 3, with sum at most n.

129
00:17:49,056 --> 00:17:58,549
Define a bijection f from A n to B n, show that f of S is in B n, and state the inverse. Pause the video and try it.

130
00:17:58,999 --> 00:18:08,002
Take n equals 9 and S equals 2, 5, 6. Sort the elements: s 1 is 2, s 2 is 5, and s 3 is 6.

131
00:18:08,452 --> 00:18:16,260
Measure the gaps, starting from 0. b 1 is the gap from 0 to s 1: 2 minus 0, which is 2.

132
00:18:16,710 --> 00:18:21,723
b 2 is the gap from s 1 to s 2: 5 minus 2, which is 3.

133
00:18:22,173 --> 00:18:27,528
b 3 is the gap from s 2 to s 3: 6 minus 5, which is 1.

134
00:18:27,978 --> 00:18:38,197
The gaps add up to s 3: 2 plus 3 plus 1 is 6. And 6 is at most n, which is 9. So the triple 2, 3, 1 is in B 9.

135
00:18:38,647 --> 00:18:44,812
So define f of S as s 1, s 2 minus s 1, s 3 minus s 2.

136
00:18:45,262 --> 00:18:55,651
The question asks you to illustrate f on the lists A 5 and B 5. The left column is A 5: the 10 subsets of 1 to 5 with 3 elements.

137
00:18:56,101 --> 00:19:01,883
The right column is B 5, in the order printed in the question. So the arrows cross.

138
00:19:02,333 --> 00:19:17,629
First, the subsets that start with 1, 2. For 1, 2, 3, f gives 1, 2 minus 1, 3 minus 2, which is 1, 1, 1. 1 2 4 goes to 1 1 2, and 1 2 5 goes to 1 1 3.

139
00:19:18,079 --> 00:19:26,783
Next, 1 3 4 goes to 1 2 1. 1 3 5 goes to 1 2 2. 1 4 5 goes to 1 3 1.

140
00:19:27,233 --> 00:19:38,753
Last, 2 3 4 goes to 2 1 1, 2 3 5 goes to 2 1 2, 2 4 5 goes to 2 2 1, and 3 4 5 goes to 3 1 1.

141
00:19:39,203 --> 00:19:45,432
There are 10 subsets and 10 triples. Every triple of B 5 gets exactly one arrow.

142
00:19:45,882 --> 00:19:58,789
Here is the written solution. Define f: for S equals s 1, s 2, s 3 in increasing order, f of S is s 1, s 2 minus s 1, s 3 minus s 2.

143
00:19:59,239 --> 00:20:14,663
Show that f of S is in B n. s 1 is at least 1. The elements are integers and s 2 is larger than s 1, so s 2 minus s 1 is at least 1. The same holds for s 3 minus s 2.

144
00:20:15,113 --> 00:20:19,209
The three entries add up to s 3, which is at most n.

145
00:20:19,659 --> 00:20:28,363
State the inverse. It takes partial sums: b 1, then b 1 plus b 2, then b 1 plus b 2 plus b 3.

146
00:20:28,813 --> 00:20:33,719
Here b is 2, 2, 1. The first partial sum is b 1, which is 2.

147
00:20:34,169 --> 00:20:37,519
The second partial sum is 2 plus 2, which is 4.

148
00:20:37,969 --> 00:20:41,681
The third partial sum is 4 plus 1, which is 5.

149
00:20:42,131 --> 00:20:53,117
So f inverse of 2, 2, 1 is 2, 4, 5. Check: f of 2, 4, 5 is 2, 4 minus 2, 5 minus 4, which is 2, 2, 1.

150
00:20:53,567 --> 00:21:05,237
Each partial sum is larger than the one before, because each b i is at least 1. The last one is at most n. So we get a 3 element subset of 1 to n.

151
00:21:05,687 --> 00:21:12,513
The question does not ask for the round trips. In a full proof, you check them as in A1-3.

152
00:21:12,963 --> 00:21:21,241
To recap. A function is injective if each b has at most one arrow in, and surjective if each b has at least one.

153
00:21:21,691 --> 00:21:26,213
A bijection has exactly one, so the two sets have the same size.

154
00:21:26,663 --> 00:21:35,389
Proposition 1.11 is the working tool. If g undoes f and f undoes g, then both are bijections.

155
00:21:35,839 --> 00:21:47,231
A bijective proof has four parts. Define f, and show that f of a is in B. Define g, and show that g of b is in A. Then check both round trips.

156
00:21:47,681 --> 00:21:56,705
We used it for indicator vectors, crosses and circles, the toggle of 1, positions minus shifts, and gaps with partial sums.

157
00:21:57,155 --> 00:22:01,955
In the next video, we prove identities by counting one set in two ways.
